## Guiding Principles

- **Readability first:** code is read far more often than it is written.
- **Keep it simple:** choose the simplest solution that works (KISS).
- **Don't repeat yourself:** extract shared logic instead of copy-pasting (DRY).
- **Single responsibility:** each function, class or module does one thing well.
- **Leave it better than you found it:** small cleanups are welcome in any change.

## Programming Languages 
This project requires to have API and our own database, so we decide to use PHP, as well as, HTML, CSS and JavaScript for dynamic web-development.
- **PHP: ** 'snake_case' for functions and variables, declaring parameter and return types, **always use prepared statements**, escape output with 'htmlspecialchars()' when printing to HTMl,
            validate input on the server even if JavaScript also validates it, keep the logic in functions that can be unit tested.
- **HTML, CSS, JavaScript: ** semantic HTML, keep the CSS and JS in different folders, 'const'/'let' for JavaScript names.

## Naming Conventions

| Item                | Convention           | Example                |
|---------------------|----------------------|------------------------|
| Variables/functions | `camelCase`          | `getUserProfile`       |
| Classes/components  | `PascalCase`         | `UserProfile`          |
| Constants           | `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE`   | `MAX_LOGIN_ATTEMPTS`   |
| Files               | `kebab-case`         | `user-profile.js`      |
| CSS classes         | `kebab-case`         | `.card-header`         |

- Use descriptive names. Prefer `userEmail` over `ue`.
- Booleans should read as questions: `isActive`, `hasAccess`.
- Avoid abbreviations unless they are widely understood.

## Formatting and Style

- Indentation: 2 spaces (no tabs).
- Maximum line length: 100 characters.
- Use semicolons and quotes consistently, as set by the linter.
- One statement per line; one blank line between logical blocks.
- Run the formatter and linter before every commit.

## Comments and Documentation

- Explain **why**, not **what**. The code shows what it does.
- Document every public function with its purpose, parameters and return value.
- Remove commented-out code; version control keeps the history.
- Mark unfinished work with `TODO:` plus your name or a ticket reference.

## Error Handling

- Handle errors explicitly; never swallow them silently.
- Fail early and give clear, actionable error messages.
- Never expose stack traces or internal details to end users.
- Validate all input at the boundary of your system.

## Version Control

### Branches

- `main`: stable, always deployable.
- `feature/<short-description>`: new work.
- `fix/<short-description>`: bug fixes.

### Commit Messages

Use the imperative mood and keep the first line under 72 characters:

```
Add login form validation
Fix crash when profile image is missing
```

- Make small, focused commits.
- Never commit secrets, credentials or large generated files.

## Code Review

- Every change goes through a pull request and needs at least one approval.
- Keep pull requests small and describe what changed and why.
- Reviewers: be constructive and specific. Authors: respond to every comment.
- Check that tests pass and the linter is clean before requesting review.

## Testing

- New features and bug fixes include tests.
- Tests are independent, repeatable and clearly named (`it_returns_error_for_empty_email`).
- Aim for meaningful coverage of core logic rather than a coverage number alone.
- All tests must pass before merging.

## Security

- Store secrets in environment variables, never in source code.
- Sanitise and validate user input to prevent injection attacks.
- Keep dependencies up to date and review new ones before adding.
- Apply the principle of least privilege to accounts, tokens and permissions.

## Definition of Done

A change is done when:

- [ ] It meets the acceptance criteria
- [ ] It follows this document
- [ ] Linting and formatting pass
- [ ] Tests are added and passing
- [ ] Documentation is updated
- [ ] It has been reviewed and approved
